\textbf{Claim.}
The fluctuation spectrum is controlled by the infrared endpoint rather than by the common ultraviolet asymptotics. All three cases share the ultraviolet behaviour
\[
V(u)\simeq \frac{15}{4u^2}\qquad (u\to0),
\]
but they separate as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{ccl}
\text{finite capped endpoint} &\Longrightarrow& \text{discrete spectrum},\\
(\epsilon,q_1,q_2)=(+1,0,0) &\Longrightarrow& \text{continuous spectrum with }M^2\ge \ell^2/L^4,\\
\ell=0,
\ F(z)=1 &\Longrightarrow& \text{continuous spectrum with }M^2\ge0.
\end{array}
\]
Using \(\eta=-\epsilon\ell^2/L^2\), the zero-gauge-field ten-dimensional D3-distribution slice with \(\epsilon=+1\) is therefore the \(\eta<0\) mass-gapped continuous case, while pure AdS is the gapless limit.

\textbf{Premises.}
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(P1)}\quad &-\frac{d^2\Psi}{du^2}+V(u)\Psi=M^2\Psi.\\
\text{(P2)}\quad &u\to0\Longrightarrow V(u)\simeq\frac{15}{4u^2},\text{ with the regular ultraviolet choice }C_2=0.\\
\text{(P3)}\quad &z_0\neq0\Longrightarrow u\to u_{\max}<\infty\text{ at the infrared endpoint}.\\
\text{(P4)}\quad &(\epsilon,q_1,q_2)=(+1,0,0)\Longrightarrow u\in(0,\infty),
\qquad \lim_{u\to\infty}V(u)=\frac{\ell^2}{L^4}.\\
\text{(P5)}\quad &\ell=0,
\ F(z)=1\Longrightarrow u\in(0,\infty),
\qquad V(u)=\frac{15}{4u^2},
\qquad \lim_{u\to\infty}V(u)=0.\\
\text{(P6)}\quad &\eta=-\epsilon\frac{\ell^2}{L^2},
\qquad L>0,
\qquad \ell>0.\\
\text{(P7)}\quad &\text{A regular Sturm--Liouville problem on a finite interval has discrete eigenvalues,}\\
&\text{while an infinite interval with }V(u)\to V_\infty\text{ has continuum threshold }M^2\ge V_\infty.
\end{aligned}
\]

\textbf{Derivation.}
The ultraviolet data in (P2) are identical in the three cases, so the ultraviolet endpoint alone cannot distinguish the spectra. The distinction comes from the infrared endpoint.
If \(z_0\neq0\), then by (P3) the radial interval is finite:
\begin{align}
u&\in(0,u_{\max}).
\end{align}
With the regular endpoint condition at \(u=0\) and the infrared regularity condition at \(u=u_{\max}\), (P7) gives a discrete sequence
\begin{align}
M&=M_n.
\end{align}
For the zero-gauge-field scalar-deformed case with \((\epsilon,q_1,q_2)=(+1,0,0)\), (P4) gives an infinite interval and
\begin{align}
V_\infty&=\frac{\ell^2}{L^4}.
\end{align}
Therefore (P7) gives a continuous spectrum with threshold
\begin{align}
M^2&\ge\frac{\ell^2}{L^4}. \label{eq:mass-gap}
\end{align}
Using (P6) with \(\epsilon=+1\),
\begin{align}
\eta&=-\frac{\ell^2}{L^2}<0,
\end{align}
so this mass-gapped continuous case is the \(\eta<0\) scalar-deformed D3-distribution slice.
For pure AdS, (P5) gives
\begin{align}
V_\infty&=0,
\end{align}
so (P7) gives a continuous spectrum with threshold
\begin{align}
M^2&\ge0. \label{eq:gapless}
\end{align}
Thus the capped soliton, the scalar-deformed D3-distribution slice, and pure AdS are spectrally inequivalent even though they share the same ultraviolet inverse-square leading potential.

\textbf{Final Result.}
\[
\boxed{z_0\neq0\Rightarrow M=M_n,
\qquad
(\epsilon,q_1,q_2)=(+1,0,0)\Rightarrow M^2\ge\frac{\ell^2}{L^4}\text{ and }\eta<0,
\qquad
\ell=0\Rightarrow M^2\ge0.}
\]
\hfill$\blacksquare$
